<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portolá expedition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1769">1769</a>–<a href="/years/1770">1770</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portolá expedition was a Spanish overland expedition led by Gaspar de Portolá in 1769–1770 to occupy Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, prompted by fears of Russian and British advances on the Pacific coast. Accompanied by the Franciscan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junipero-serra">Junípero Serra</a>, it founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a>, and its scouts became the first Europeans known to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> Bay. In 1770 Portolá established the presidio of Monterey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portola-expedition.jpg" alt="Portolá expedition" width="320" height="367" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristiano Tomás · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Portol%C3%A1_Expedition_of_California_(1769-1770).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> coast since Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo's voyage of 1542, and Sebastián Vizcaíno had surveyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> and Monterey in 1602, but for some 160 years afterwards little was done to settle the region by land. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767, Gaspar de Portolá, a Catalan officer, became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-californias">Las Californias</a>. The visitador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez">José de Gálvez</a> planned a joint movement by land and sea, with the ships supplying the land parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Three ships and two overland parties left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> early in 1769. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> and San Carlos reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> in April, their crews badly weakened by scurvy, and the San José was lost at sea. Captain Fernando Rivera's column arrived on 14 May and Portolá's party, with Serra, on 1 July. Of the more than 90 men of the sea expedition, only eight soldiers and eight sailors remained fit.</p><p>On 14 July Portolá marched north with 74 men. The party reached Monterey Bay on 1 October but did not recognise it as the port Vizcaíno had described. Continuing north, Sergeant José Francisco Ortega's scouts found their way blocked by a vast bay, and the friar Juan Crespí wrote that it could shelter the navies of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. An officers' council on 11 November decided to turn back, and the party, reduced to eating seagulls, pelicans and a mule, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> on 24 January 1770.</p><p>A second, smaller party left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a> on 17 April 1770 and reached Monterey Bay on 24 May, finding the cross it had planted the previous December surrounded by feathers, arrows and offerings of food. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> arrived on 31 May with Serra, who founded the Mission San Carlos Borromeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The parties had travelled around 1,200 miles and reported large numbers of friendly Indians along the coast; contacts were mostly peaceful, eased by the glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and trinkets the Spanish carried. With the later de Anza expedition, the route became the Camino Real, along which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> founded twenty-one missions. Diaries by Portolá, the engineer Miguel Costansó and Crespí survive, and Pedro Fages became lieutenant governor of Alta California at Monterey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portolá expedition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fearing Russian and British advances on the Pacific coast, Spain sent the <b>Portolá expedition</b> north in 1769.</blockquote><a href="/state/alta-california">Alta California</a></li></ul></section>
